<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net472</TargetFrameworks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- The doc comments ARE the contract's documentation: an Uplink author
         hovering a channel type is reading these, the same prose
         `scripts/asyncapi/prose-hygiene.mjs` keeps client-facing. Shipping the
         XML beside the assembly is what makes that hover work outside this
         repo; `KspGonogo.Sitrep.Contract` packs it into every lib folder.

         ONLY CS1591 (missing doc on a public member) is suppressed. A
         completeness ratchet over ~700 public members is a separate decision
         from packaging the XML, and `scripts/asyncapi/prose-hygiene.mjs` is the
         ratchet this prose actually answers to.

         Everything else is ON, which buys the thing a rename sweep structurally
         cannot see: a cref to a member that is removed or renamed becomes a
         warning in the same commit that removes it. Three such defects were
         sitting in the published contract when the family was first turned on,
         one of them pointing an Uplink author at a retired interface.

         THE RULE THAT COMES WITH IT: name anything BELOW this assembly in the
         dependency graph with `<c>Name</c>`, never `<see cref="Name"/>`.
         Sitrep.Contract is the bottom of the graph, so a cref to Sitrep.Host's
         ChannelEngine, Sitrep.Core's Courier, Sitrep.Propagation's
         KeplerProvider, or an Uplink's payload type cannot resolve BY
         CONSTRUCTION. It has never been a link for anybody, and `<c>` renders
         the same. Without this rule the next perfectly good `<internal>`
         paragraph reintroduces a warning by construction. The same goes for a
         changelog entry naming a type that has since left the contract: it
         documents history, so it takes `<c>` too. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);1591</NoWarn>
    <!-- SourceLink, so a stepped-into contract type resolves to the commit it
         was built from. The .NET SDK has carried Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub
         since 8.0, so these properties are the whole wiring and no
         PackageReference is added: see this file's Reinforced.Typings header
         for why a reference here is never casual. -->
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <DebugType>portable</DebugType>
    <!-- Sitrep.Contract is the public Uplink surface, and MIT like the rest
         of gonogo (see LICENSING.md). Third-party Uplinks under ANY license
         (GPL, proprietary, ARR, etc.) can reference the contract; MIT's only
         condition is retaining the copyright/permission notice.
         GonogoKosUplink is the one GPL-3.0-only assembly in the repo, it links
         kOS: but it is a leaf and imposes nothing on this or any other
         assembly. -->
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!-- NO Reinforced.Typings reference here, in ANY target framework, and
       nothing may add one back.

       Codegen needs the [TsInterface]/[TsEnum] attributes in an assembly's
       metadata, but a shipped assembly that carries them also carries a hard
       metadata reference to Reinforced.Typings.dll, which is deliberately
       never deployed beside it. Every consumer then breaks the moment it asks
       a contract type for its attributes, and Enum.ToString() asks: enum
       formatting looks for [Flags]. That turned situation.ToString() in a
       net10.0 test into a FileNotFoundException, and took xunit's rendering of
       the failure down with it, so the assertion's own message threw. It also
       threatened the net472 KSP build, where Kopernicus reflects over every
       loaded assembly at startup.

       PrivateAssets="all" did NOT prevent this. It governs what NuGet
       propagates to REFERENCING projects; it says nothing about what the
       compiler bakes into THIS assembly's metadata. The reference was private
       and the breakage was total, which is why the fix had to move the
       attributes out of the shipped assembly rather than tune asset flow.

       The RT attributes and RtConfig now live behind #if SITREP_CODEGEN and
       are compiled only by ../Sitrep.Contract.Codegen, which recompiles these
       same sources purely as rtcli's input. Every Uplink contract slice has a
       matching .Codegen twin, all sharing ../CodegenTwin.props.

       Guarded by Sitrep.Core.Tests/ContractEnumRenderingTests. -->
  <!-- Sitrep.Core's ChannelEmitter constructs EmissionDecision/EmissionCounters
       via their internal factory methods/constructors (EmissionDecision.Emit/
       Skip, EmissionCounters' 2-arg ctor): deliberately internal so nothing
       outside the emitter can fabricate a decision/counters snapshot.
       EmissionPolicy and its EmissionDecision/EmissionCounters siblings moved
       here from Sitrep.Core during the Uplink-foundation review's fix round
       (see UplinkContract.cs's ISitrepUplink doc comment), so that
       cross-assembly internal access needs this friend-assembly grant now,
       same pattern Sitrep.Core.csproj already uses for Sitrep.Host. -->
  <!-- Sitrep.Host writes recorded-session JSON through Serialization's
       JsonWriter, which is internal and stays internal: the wire FORMAT is the
       public surface here (EnvelopeCodec, BinaryFrameCodec), the writer behind
       it is not. The grant moved with the serializer out of Sitrep.Core, which
       carried the identical one for the identical reason. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Sitrep.Core" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Sitrep.Host" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
